          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is cognitive behavioural therapy described as first-line for insomnia rather than medication?",
          "answer": "Clinical guidance from sleep medicine and internal medicine societies identifies cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) as the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in adults, prioritizing it over medication (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line). Medication is generally considered a second-line, time-limited option (condition-insomnia).\n\nThe preference for CBT-I is based on several factors:\n\n*   **Efficacy and Long-Term Outcomes**: The benefits of CBT-I persist even after the treatment concludes. In contrast, the effects of pharmacological treatments typically do not last once the medication is stopped (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line). Chronic insomnia responds durably to non-drug treatments, which helps most people avoid long-term dependence on hypnotic medications (condition-insomnia).\n*   **Side Effects**: CBT-I helps patients avoid the potential harms and side effects associated with the long-term use of hypnotic medications (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line).\n\nCBT-I typically involves four to eight sessions and includes components such as stimulus control (using the bed only for sleep and leaving it when unable to sleep) and sleep restriction (limiting time in bed to actual sleep time and then gradually increasing it as sleep improves) (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
